  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 6.05 — Information Geometry\n\nEverything so far has been geometry of *space*. This notebook finds the same machinery in a place with no space in it at all: the set of probability distributions.\n\n**The manifold.** Fix a parametrised family $p(x \\mid \\theta)$ — Gaussians with $\\theta = (\\mu,\\sigma)$, Bernoullis with $\\theta = p$, and so on. The parameter space is a manifold, and a point of it *is* a distribution.\n\n**The Fisher metric.** Define\n\n$$g_{ij}(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}\\Big[\\frac{\\partial \\log p}{\\partial\\theta^i}\\frac{\\partial \\log p}{\\partial\\theta^j}\\Big] = -\\,\\mathbb{E}\\Big[\\frac{\\partial^2 \\log p}{\\partial\\theta^i\\partial\\theta^j}\\Big].$$\n\nIt is symmetric and positive semi-definite by construction, so it is a Riemannian metric — and it is the essentially unique metric invariant under sufficient statistics (Chentsov's theorem). That uniqueness is what makes this geometry rather than an analogy.\n\n**Divergence as squared distance.** The Kullback–Leibler divergence\n\n$$D(p_\\theta \\,\\|\\, p_{\\theta+d\\theta}) = \\tfrac12\\,g_{ij}\\,d\\theta^i d\\theta^j + O(|d\\theta|^3)$$\n\nis *not* symmetric and *not* a metric — but its second-order expansion is exactly the Fisher metric. **The geometry is the infinitesimal shadow of the divergence.**\n\n**Two flat connections.** Information geometry's distinctive feature is that it uses connections which are *not* Levi-Civita. There is a one-parameter family $\\nabla^{(\\alpha)}$, with\n\n- $\\alpha = +1$: the **exponential** connection, flat on exponential families,\n- $\\alpha = -1$: the **mixture** connection, flat on mixture families,\n- $\\alpha = 0$: Levi-Civita for the Fisher metric.\n\n$\\nabla^{(1)}$ and $\\nabla^{(-1)}$ are **dual** with respect to $g$: $Xg(Y,Z) = g(\\nabla^{(1)}_XY, Z) + g(Y, \\nabla^{(-1)}_XZ)$. Stage 4 only ever met the metric-compatible case, where a connection is its own dual. Here the pair carries the structure, and a space can be flat for one while curved for the other.\n\n**Where this pays off.** Natural gradient descent ($\\theta \\leftarrow \\theta - \\eta\\,g^{-1}\\nabla L$) is gradient descent done with the right metric rather than the coordinate one, and is invariant to reparametrisation. The Cramér–Rao bound is the statement that $g^{-1}$ lower-bounds estimator covariance. The EM algorithm is alternating projection between the two flat structures.\n\n**The connection back to Stage 3.** The space of Gaussians with the Fisher metric turns out to be the **hyperbolic plane** — 2.06's upper half-plane, with $K = -1/2$. Statistics and non-Euclidean geometry meet, and this notebook computes the curvature to check it.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nassume(sg > 0)$\n\n/* --- The Fisher metric of the univariate Gaussian --- */\npdf  : exp(-(xx - mu)^2/(2*sg^2)) / (sg*sqrt(2*%pi))$\nlp   : -(xx-mu)^2/(2*sg^2) - log(sg) - log(2*%pi)/2$\nprint(\"log p(x | μ,σ) = \", lp)$\nprint(\"   check against log(pdf): \", radcan( lp - log(pdf) ),\n      \"   (radcan, not ratsimp: the check mixes logs and exponentials)\")$\n\nth : [mu, sg]$\n/* Expectations: integrate in the CENTRED variable y = x − μ, so that the\n   integrand carries no μ and Maxima does not stop to ask whether μ > 0. */\nEfun(expr) := ratsimp( integrate(\n    subst(xx = mu + yv, expr) * exp(-yv^2/(2*sg^2))/(sg*sqrt(2*%pi)),\n    yv, minf, inf) )$\n\ngF : genmatrix( lambda([i,j],\n       Efun( diff(lp, th[i]) * diff(lp, th[j]) )), 2, 2)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Fisher metric  g_ij = E[∂ᵢlog p · ∂ⱼlog p] :\")$\nprint(\"   g = \", gF)$\n\n/* the second formula for the same thing */\ngF2 : genmatrix( lambda([i,j], Efun( -diff(lp, th[i], 1, th[j], 1) )), 2, 2)$\nprint(\"   and  g_ij = −E[∂ᵢ∂ⱼ log p] :  difference = \", ratsimp(gF - gF2))$\nprint(\"   The two formulas agree because the score has mean zero, which is\")$\nprint(\"   just ∫∂ᵢp dx = ∂ᵢ∫p dx = ∂ᵢ1 = 0 :\")$\nprint(\"      E[∂ᵢ log p] = \", makelist( Efun(diff(lp, th[i])), i, 1, 2))$\n\n/* --- and it is the hyperbolic plane --- */\nts(e) := ratsimp(trigsimp(e))$\nchristoffel_hand(g, vars) := block([ginv, n_],\n  n_ : length(vars),  ginv : ratsimp(invert(g)),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(g[j][l], vars[i]) + diff(g[i][l], vars[j])\n                        - diff(g[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, n_)/2 ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_), k,1,n_) )$\nriem_hand(g, vars) := block([G, n_],\n  G : christoffel_hand(g, vars), n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( diff(G[l][j][k], vars[i]) - diff(G[l][i][k], vars[j])\n        + sum( G[l][i][m]*G[m][j][k] - G[l][j][m]*G[m][i][k], m, 1, n_) ),\n    k,1,n_), j,1,n_), i,1,n_), l,1,n_) )$\nriem_low_hand(g, vars) := block([Rr, n_],\n  Rr : riem_hand(g, vars), n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( g[l][m]*Rr[m][i][j][k], m,1,n_) ),\n    l,1,n_), k,1,n_), j,1,n_), i,1,n_) )$\nK2d(g, vars) := block([Rm], Rm : riem_low_hand(g, vars),\n  ts( Rm[1][2][2][1] / determinant(g) ) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"THE SPACE OF GAUSSIANS IS HYPERBOLIC.\")$\nprint(\"   ds² = dμ²/σ² + 2dσ²/σ²\")$\nprint(\"   K = \", K2d(gF, [mu, sg]))$\nprint(\"   Negative and constant.  Rescaling μ = √2·u turns the metric into\")$\nprint(\"   2(du² + dσ²)/σ², which is 2× the upper half-plane metric of 2.06.\")$\nprint(\"   Scaling a metric by c scales K by 1/c, so K = −1/2 ✓.\")$\ngcheck : matrix([2/sg^2, 0],[0, 2/sg^2])$\nprint(\"   check: K of 2(du² + dσ²)/σ² = \", K2d(gcheck, [uu, sg]))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"So the statistician's space of normal distributions and 2.06's\")$\nprint(\"hyperbolic plane are the same surface.  Distributions differing in\")$\nprint(\"σ are hyperbolically far apart, and σ → 0 is the boundary at\")$\nprint(\"infinity — infinitely distant, which is one geometric reason\")$\nprint(\"estimating a near-zero variance is hard.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "log p(x | μ,σ) =  -((xx-mu)^2/(2*sg^2))-log(sg)-log(2*%pi)/2\n   check against log(pdf):  0\n                              (radcan, not ratsimp: the check mixes logs and exponentials)\nFisher metric  g_ij = E[∂ᵢlog p · ∂ⱼlog p] :\n   g =  matrix([1/sg^2,0],[0,2/sg^2])\n   and  g_ij = −E[∂ᵢ∂ⱼ log p] :  difference =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   The two formulas agree because the score has mean zero, which is\n   just ∫∂ᵢp dx = ∂ᵢ∫p dx = ∂ᵢ1 = 0 :\n      E[∂ᵢ log p] =  [0,0]\nTHE SPACE OF GAUSSIANS IS HYPERBOLIC.\n   ds² = dμ²/σ² + 2dσ²/σ²\n   K =  -(1/2)\n   Negative and constant.  Rescaling μ = √2·u turns the metric into\n   2(du² + dσ²)/σ², which is 2× the upper half-plane metric of 2.06.\n   Scaling a metric by c scales K by 1/c, so K = −1/2 ✓.\n   check: K of 2(du² + dσ²)/σ² =  -(1/2)\nSo the statistician's space of normal distributions and 2.06's\nhyperbolic plane are the same surface.  Distributions differing in\nσ are hyperbolically far apart, and σ → 0 is the boundary at\ninfinity — infinitely distant, which is one geometric reason\nestimating a near-zero variance is hard."
          ]
        }
