    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- KL divergence, and why the Fisher metric is its second derivative --- */\nassume(s1 > 0, s2 > 0)$\nlp1 : -(xx-m1)^2/(2*s1^2) - log(s1) - log(2*%pi)/2$\nlp2 : -(xx-m2)^2/(2*s2^2) - log(s2) - log(2*%pi)/2$\nE1(expr) := ratsimp( integrate(\n    subst(xx = m1 + yv, expr) * exp(-yv^2/(2*s1^2))/(s1*sqrt(2*%pi)),\n    yv, minf, inf) )$\nKLcomp : ratsimp( E1(lp1 - lp2) )$\nKLform : log(s2/s1) + (s1^2 + (m1-m2)^2)/(2*s2^2) - 1/2$\nprint(\"KL(p₁ ‖ p₂) = E₁[log p₁ − log p₂], computed by integration:\")$\nprint(\"   \", KLcomp)$\nprint(\"   against the closed form log(σ₂/σ₁) + (σ₁²+(μ₁−μ₂)²)/(2σ₂²) − ½ :\")$\nprint(\"   difference = \", radcan( KLcomp - KLform ))$\n\n/* not symmetric, not a metric */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"NOT SYMMETRIC.  With p₁ = N(0,1) and p₂ = N(1,2):\")$\nkl12 : subst([m1=0, s1=1, m2=1, s2=2], KLform)$\nkl21 : subst([m1=1, s1=2, m2=0, s2=1], KLform)$\nprint(\"   D(p₁‖p₂) = \", float(kl12))$\nprint(\"   D(p₂‖p₁) = \", float(kl21))$\nprint(\"   D is not a distance.  It has no triangle inequality either.\")$\n\n/* --- but its second-order expansion IS the metric --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"SECOND ORDER.  Put μ₂ = μ + αs, σ₂ = σ + βs and expand in s:\")$\nKLnear : subst([m1 = mu, s1 = sg, m2 = mu + al*ss, s2 = sg + be*ss], KLform)$\nKLtay : ratdisrep( taylor(KLnear, ss, 0, 2) )$\nprint(\"   D = \", ratsimp(KLtay))$\nhalf_g : ratsimp( (gF[1][1]*al^2 + 2*gF[1][2]*al*be + gF[2][2]*be^2)*ss^2/2 )$\nprint(\"   ½ g_ij δθⁱδθʲ = \", half_g)$\nprint(\"   difference = \", ratsimp( KLtay - half_g ))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The first-order term vanishes — D has a minimum of zero at δθ = 0 —\")$\nprint(\"and the second-order term is exactly ½ times the Fisher metric.\")$\nprint(\"A divergence with no symmetry and no triangle inequality has, as its\")$\nprint(\"infinitesimal shadow, a genuine Riemannian metric.  That is the\")$\nprint(\"whole idea: statistics supplies the divergence, geometry appears in\")$\nprint(\"its Hessian.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "KL(p₁ ‖ p₂) = E₁[log p₁ − log p₂], computed by integration:\n    (2*s2^2*log(s2)+(-(2*log(s1))-1)*s2^2+s1^2+m2^2-2*m1*m2+m1^2)/(2*s2^2)\n   against the closed form log(σ₂/σ₁) + (σ₁²+(μ₁−μ₂)²)/(2σ₂²) − ½ :\n   difference =  0\nNOT SYMMETRIC.  With p₁ = N(0,1) and p₂ = N(1,2):\n   D(p₁‖p₂) =  0.4431471805599453\n   D(p₂‖p₁) =  1.3068528194400546\n   D is not a distance.  It has no triangle inequality either.\nSECOND ORDER.  Put μ₂ = μ + αs, σ₂ = σ + βs and expand in s:\n   D =  ((2*be^2+al^2)*ss^2)/(2*sg^2)\n   ½ g_ij δθⁱδθʲ =  ((2*be^2+al^2)*ss^2)/(2*sg^2)\n   difference =  0\nThe first-order term vanishes — D has a minimum of zero at δθ = 0 —\nand the second-order term is exactly ½ times the Fisher metric.\nA divergence with no symmetry and no triangle inequality has, as its\ninfinitesimal shadow, a genuine Riemannian metric.  That is the\nwhole idea: statistics supplies the divergence, geometry appears in\nits Hessian."
