    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Discrete families: the simplex is a sphere --- */\nassume(tt > 0, tt < 1)$\nlpB : xb*log(tt) + (1-xb)*log(1-tt)$          /* Bernoulli, x ∈ {0,1} */\nEB(expr) := ratsimp( tt*subst(xb=1, expr) + (1-tt)*subst(xb=0, expr) )$\ngB : EB( diff(lpB, tt)^2 )$\nprint(\"BERNOULLI, p(x|θ) = θˣ(1−θ)¹⁻ˣ :\")$\nprint(\"   score ∂log p/∂θ = \", ratsimp(diff(lpB, tt)))$\nprint(\"   E[score] = \", EB(diff(lpB, tt)), \"   (zero, as it must be)\")$\nprint(\"   Fisher metric g = E[score²] = \", gB, \"  = 1/(θ(1−θ))\")$\nprint(\"   also −E[∂²log p] = \", EB(-diff(lpB, tt, 2)),\n      \"   difference \", ratsimp(gB - EB(-diff(lpB,tt,2))))$\n\nlenB : integrate( 1/sqrt(tt*(1-tt)), tt, 0, 1)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   total length of the family, ∫₀¹ dθ/√(θ(1−θ)) = \", lenB)$\nprint(\"   Finite.  The whole of Bernoulli statistics is a curve of length π,\")$\nprint(\"   even though θ → 0 and θ → 1 look as though they should run away.\")$\nprint(\"   (Contrast the Gaussians, where σ → 0 IS at infinite distance.)\")$\n\n/* the √p embedding */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"WHY π: the simplex is a piece of a sphere.  Put zᵢ = 2√pᵢ.\")$\nprint(\"   Σ zᵢ² = 4Σpᵢ = 4, so z lies on a sphere of radius 2.\")$\nprint(\"   And dzᵢ = dpᵢ/√pᵢ, so Σdzᵢ² = Σ dpᵢ²/pᵢ — which IS the Fisher\")$\nprint(\"   metric on the simplex.\")$\nfish_simplex : ratsimp( 1/tt + 1/(1-tt) )$\nprint(\"   two outcomes, p₁ = θ and p₂ = 1−θ :\")$\nprint(\"      Σ dpᵢ²/pᵢ = (1/θ + 1/(1−θ)) dθ² = \", fish_simplex, \" dθ²\")$\nprint(\"      equals the Fisher metric ? \", ratsimp(fish_simplex - gB))$\nprint(\"   So Bernoulli is a quarter of a great circle on a sphere of\")$\nprint(\"   radius 2, of length \", ratsimp(2*%pi*2/4), \" ✓\")$\nprint(\"   The Fisher metric on discrete distributions is the ROUND METRIC\")$\nprint(\"   of Stage 3 in disguise, and Hellinger distance is chordal\")$\nprint(\"   distance on that sphere.\")$\n\n/* --- natural gradient is a vector; the ordinary gradient is not --- */\n/* Compute each side independently in its own coordinates, then transform\n   one to the other — rather than asserting they agree. */\ndepends(LL, [mu, sg])$\ngS : matrix([1/sg^2, 0],[0, 2/sg^2])$                /* Fisher in (μ,σ) */\ngT : matrix([1/sg^2, 0],[0, 2])$                     /* Fisher in (μ,t), t = log σ,\n                                                        written with σ = eᵗ */\nJst : matrix([1, 0],[0, 1/sg])$                      /* d(μ,t)/d(μ,σ) */\ngradS : transpose(matrix([ diff(LL,mu), diff(LL,sg) ]))$\ngradT : transpose(matrix([ diff(LL,mu), sg*diff(LL,sg) ]))$   /* ∂L/∂t = σ∂L/∂σ */\n\nnatS : ratsimp( invert(gS) . gradS )$\nnatT : ratsimp( invert(gT) . gradT )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"NATURAL GRADIENT.  Reparametrise the Gaussians by t = log σ.\")$\nprint(\"   Fisher metric in (μ,σ) : \", gS)$\nprint(\"   Fisher metric in (μ,t) : \", gT, \"  (same metric, new coordinates)\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   natural gradient in (μ,σ) : \", transpose(natS))$\nprint(\"   natural gradient in (μ,t) : \", transpose(natT))$\nprint(\"   push the first forward with J = d(μ,t)/d(μ,σ) = \", Jst, \" :\")$\nprint(\"      J·natS = \", transpose( ratsimp(Jst . natS) ))$\nprint(\"      J·natS − natT = \", transpose( ratsimp(Jst . natS - natT) ),\n      \"   ✓\")$\nprint(\"   The natural gradient is a genuine tangent vector: it does not\")$\nprint(\"   care which coordinates the optimiser happens to use.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   the ORDINARY gradient, treated the same way:\")$\nprint(\"      J·gradS = \", transpose( ratsimp(Jst . gradS) ))$\nprint(\"      gradT   = \", transpose(gradT))$\nprint(\"      J·gradS − gradT = \", transpose( ratsimp(Jst . gradS - gradT) ))$\nprint(\"   Non-zero except at σ = 1.  The ordinary gradient is a COVECTOR\")$\nprint(\"   being used as a vector; raising the index needs a metric.  Using\")$\nprint(\"   δᵢⱼ means treating the coordinate system as if it were geometry.\")$\nprint(\"   That is the whole content of natural gradient descent, and it is\")$\nprint(\"   4.02's distinction between ∂ᵢVʲ and ∇ᵢVʲ once again.\")$\nremove(LL, dependency)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "BERNOULLI, p(x|θ) = θˣ(1−θ)¹⁻ˣ :\n   score ∂log p/∂θ =  -((xb-tt)/(tt^2-tt))\n   E[score] =  0    (zero, as it must be)\n   Fisher metric g = E[score²] =  -(1/(tt^2-tt))   = 1/(θ(1−θ))\n   also −E[∂²log p] =  -(1/(tt^2-tt))    difference  0\n   total length of the family, ∫₀¹ dθ/√(θ(1−θ)) =  %pi\n   Finite.  The whole of Bernoulli statistics is a curve of length π,\n   even though θ → 0 and θ → 1 look as though they should run away.\n   (Contrast the Gaussians, where σ → 0 IS at infinite distance.)\nWHY π: the simplex is a piece of a sphere.  Put zᵢ = 2√pᵢ.\n   Σ zᵢ² = 4Σpᵢ = 4, so z lies on a sphere of radius 2.\n   And dzᵢ = dpᵢ/√pᵢ, so Σdzᵢ² = Σ dpᵢ²/pᵢ — which IS the Fisher\n   metric on the simplex.\n   two outcomes, p₁ = θ and p₂ = 1−θ :\n      Σ dpᵢ²/pᵢ = (1/θ + 1/(1−θ)) dθ² =  -(1/(tt^2-tt))  dθ²\n      equals the Fisher metric ?  0\n   So Bernoulli is a quarter of a great circle on a sphere of\n   radius 2, of length  %pi  ✓\n   The Fisher metric on discrete distributions is the ROUND METRIC\n   of Stage 3 in disguise, and Hellinger distance is chordal\n   distance on that sphere.\nNATURAL GRADIENT.  Reparametrise the Gaussians by t = log σ.\n   Fisher metric in (μ,σ) :  matrix([1/sg^2,0],[0,2/sg^2])\n   Fisher metric in (μ,t) :  matrix([1/sg^2,0],[0,2])\n                              (same metric, new coordinates)\n   natural gradient in (μ,σ) :\n                               matrix(['diff(LL,mu,1)*sg^2,\n                                       ('diff(LL,sg,1)*sg^2)/2])\n   natural gradient in (μ,t) :\n                               matrix(['diff(LL,mu,1)*sg^2,\n                                       ('diff(LL,sg,1)*sg)/2])\n   push the first forward with J = d(μ,t)/d(μ,σ) =  matrix([1,0],[0,1/sg])  :\n      J·natS =  matrix(['diff(LL,mu,1)*sg^2,('diff(LL,sg,1)*sg)/2])\n      J·natS − natT =  matrix([0,0])    ✓\n   The natural gradient is a genuine tangent vector: it does not\n   care which coordinates the optimiser happens to use.\n   the ORDINARY gradient, treated the same way:\n      J·gradS =  matrix(['diff(LL,mu,1),'diff(LL,sg,1)/sg])\n      gradT   =  matrix(['diff(LL,mu,1),'diff(LL,sg,1)*sg])\n      J·gradS − gradT =\n                        matrix([0,-(('diff(LL,sg,1)*sg^2-'diff(LL,sg,1))/sg)])\n   Non-zero except at σ = 1.  The ordinary gradient is a COVECTOR\n   being used as a vector; raising the index needs a metric.  Using\n   δᵢⱼ means treating the coordinate system as if it were geometry.\n   That is the whole content of natural gradient descent, and it is\n   4.02's distinction between ∂ᵢVʲ and ∇ᵢVʲ once again."
          ]
        }
