    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$g_{ij} = \\mathbb{E}\\big[\\partial_i\\log p\\,\\partial_j\\log p\\big], \\qquad D(p_\\theta\\|p_{\\theta+\\delta}) = \\tfrac12 g_{ij}\\delta^i\\delta^j + O(\\delta^3)$$\n\n**The Fisher metric, computed.** For the univariate Gaussian, integrating in the centred variable gives\n\n$$g = \\begin{pmatrix} 1/\\sigma^2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2/\\sigma^2\\end{pmatrix},$$\n\nand the two standard formulas — $\\mathbb{E}[\\partial_i\\log p\\,\\partial_j\\log p]$ and $-\\mathbb{E}[\\partial_i\\partial_j\\log p]$ — agreed with difference zero, because $\\mathbb{E}[\\partial_i\\log p] = 0$ (also computed: $[0,0]$).\n\n**The space of Gaussians is the hyperbolic plane.** $K = -1/2$, computed with 4.04's machinery. Rescaling $\\mu = \\sqrt2 u$ makes the metric $2(du^2+d\\sigma^2)/\\sigma^2$ — twice 2.06's upper half-plane, and scaling a metric by $c$ scales $K$ by $1/c$, so $-1/2$ is right. Checked independently on the rescaled form.\n\n$\\sigma \\to 0$ is the boundary at infinity: **infinitely far away in the Fisher metric**, which is one geometric reason estimating a near-zero variance is hard.\n\n**KL divergence is not a distance, and its Hessian is.** The closed form was verified by integration (difference zero), and it is visibly asymmetric: $D(N(0,1)\\|N(1,2)) = 0.443$ against $D(N(1,2)\\|N(0,1)) = 1.307$. But expanding $\\mu_2 = \\mu + \\alpha s$, $\\sigma_2 = \\sigma + \\beta s$ gives\n\n$$D = \\frac{(\\alpha^2 + 2\\beta^2)s^2}{2\\sigma^2},$$\n\nwhich matched $\\tfrac12 g_{ij}\\delta\\theta^i\\delta\\theta^j$ exactly. The first-order term vanishes because $D$ has a minimum of zero there. **A function with no symmetry and no triangle inequality has a genuine Riemannian metric as its second derivative** — that is the whole idea of the subject.\n\n**Discrete families are spheres.** Bernoulli has $g = 1/(\\theta(1-\\theta))$, and $\\int_0^1 d\\theta/\\sqrt{\\theta(1-\\theta)} = \\pi$: the entire family is a curve of **finite** length, unlike the Gaussians. The reason is the substitution $z_i = 2\\sqrt{p_i}$, which puts the simplex on a sphere of radius 2 and turns the Fisher metric into the round metric — verified for two outcomes, where $1/\\theta + 1/(1-\\theta)$ is exactly $g$. A quarter of a great circle of radius 2 has length $\\pi$.\n\nSo Stage 3's round sphere and Stage 2's hyperbolic plane both turn up in statistics, on different families.\n\n**Natural gradient is a vector; the ordinary gradient is not.** Reparametrising $t = \\log\\sigma$ and computing each side independently in its own coordinates:\n\n| | pushed forward from $(\\mu,\\sigma)$ vs computed in $(\\mu,t)$ |\n|---|---|\n| natural gradient $g^{-1}\\nabla L$ | difference $[0,0]$ |\n| ordinary gradient $\\nabla L$ | difference $\\big[0, -(\\sigma^2-1)\\sigma^{-1}\\partial_\\sigma L\\big]$ |\n\nThe ordinary gradient agrees only at $\\sigma = 1$ — by coincidence of the parametrisation. It is a **covector being used as a vector**, and raising the index requires a metric; using $\\delta_{ij}$ means treating the coordinate system as if it were the geometry. That is 4.02's distinction between $\\partial_iV^j$ and $\\nabla_iV^j$, arriving in machine learning.\n\n**What is different here.** Stage 4 always used the Levi-Civita connection, which is its own dual. Information geometry uses a *pair* of flat connections — exponential and mixture — dual with respect to $g$, with Levi-Civita as their midpoint $\\alpha = 0$. A family can be flat for one and curved for the other, and that asymmetry is what the EM algorithm and maximum likelihood are exploiting. This notebook computes the metric side only; the dual connections are stated, not verified.\n\n## Next\n\n**6.06 — Discrete Differential Geometry** rebuilds Stages 3 and 4 on triangle meshes, where curvature becomes angle defect and Gauss–Bonnet holds **exactly** rather than to discretisation error — the last place in the journey where a continuous theorem survives digitisation unchanged.\n\n## References\n\n- Amari, S. and Nagaoka, H. (2000) *Methods of Information Geometry*. Translations of Mathematical Monographs 191. Providence: American Mathematical Society. The standard reference; Chapters 2–3 for the metric and the $\\alpha$-connections, Chapter 5 for the Gaussian case computed above.\n- Amari, S. (2016) *Information Geometry and Its Applications*. Applied Mathematical Sciences 194. Tokyo: Springer. The later and more accessible book, and the source for natural gradient descent as an optimisation method.\n- Rao, C.R. (1945) 'Information and the accuracy attainable in the estimation of statistical parameters', *Bulletin of the Calcutta Mathematical Society*, 37, pp. 81–91. Where the Fisher information is first treated as a Riemannian metric.\n- Čencov, N.N. (1982) *Statistical Decision Rules and Optimal Inference*. Translations of Mathematical Monographs 53. Providence: American Mathematical Society. The uniqueness theorem: the Fisher metric is essentially the only metric invariant under sufficient statistics, which is what makes this geometry rather than analogy.\n- Nielsen, F. (2020) 'An elementary introduction to information geometry', *Entropy*, 22(10), 1100. [doi:10.3390/e22101100](https://doi.org/10.3390/e22101100). Open access, and the gentlest entry point.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library; none of this notebook's references are held locally, so all are given from knowledge and were not consulted. Every code cell was executed. The dual $\\alpha$-connections are described in the header but not computed — only the Fisher metric and its Levi-Civita curvature are verified here. One Maxima note: expectations are integrated in the centred variable $y = x-\\mu$, because otherwise `integrate` stops to ask whether $\\mu$ is positive.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}